---
// The brief's "ticket-card" module (PROJECT_BRIEF.md Section 3 principle 5
// and Section 7): one component, reused everywhere a project thumbnail
// appears (homepage Selected Work now, Work index later). Content-agnostic
// via props, so it lives in ui/ rather than sections/.
//
// Case-study pages are PROPOSED, not built yet (PROJECT_BRIEF.md Section
// 5) - see the accessibility-rebuild comment below for why this no
// longer takes an `href` at all.
//
// Rebuilt around a full-bleed image with a hover-reveal info panel — per
// direct request ("the image to fill the entire card at first, numbers in
// the left corners can stay, and then it reveals the part with the info
// while hovering"). This directly reverses an earlier direct request that
// the full title stay visible at all times - a live instruction always
// supersedes an earlier one when they conflict, and this one was explicit
// about the mechanism (image first, info on hover), not just a copy tweak.
//
// This replaces the previous image-frame/info-body split (a fixed aspect
// ratio frame stacked over a separate padded body holding stub/tag/year/
// title/arrow) with one element: the whole card *is* the image, and
// `.project-card__overlay` is an absolutely-positioned panel sitting at
// its bottom edge, translated fully out of view (`translateY(100%)`) at
// rest and sliding up into view on `:hover` (see the accessibility-
// rebuild comment below for why this is hover-only now, not
// `:focus-visible` too). This isn't
// just a visual change - it's what actually fixes the collapsed-accordion
// clipping bug that kept resurfacing (see AGENTS.md for the three earlier
// attempts, each just enlarging a fixed writing-mode budget). That bug's
// entire root cause - a spine title rotated into `writing-mode:
// vertical-rl` inside a fixed-height column budget - no longer exists:
// there's no separate "collapsed" visual state to maintain at all now.
// SelectedWork.astro's accordion just resizes this same card via
// flex-grow; the card's own :hover already fires the moment the mouse is
// over it (which is also when the panel is mid-expansion), so the overlay
// and the accordion's width change happen together with no extra wiring.
//
// Touch has no hover to reveal the overlay with, so under `(hover: none)`
// the card drops out of the overlay pattern entirely: the image gets a
// fixed aspect-ratio and sits in normal flow above a permanently-visible,
// non-overlapping overlay (`position: static`) - functionally the same
// "always show the facts, no gesture required" fallback this project
// already uses for the arrow-vs-cursor split, just applied to the whole
// info panel instead of one icon.
//
// Deliberately no layout-mode props (an earlier `featured` prop, for a
// now-abandoned bento layout, was removed rather than left unused) - any
// layout-specific presentation (SelectedWork's accordion strip included)
// is applied from outside via `:global()` overrides scoped to that
// section's own class, not by teaching this component about every
// section that might reuse it.
//
// Data contract rebuilt around projects.json (see SelectedWork.astro's
// own comment): `tag` is "UX" or "Visual" (the site's new filter
// categories, replacing the old free-text `roleTag`), plus `oneLiner`,
// `team` and `role` - all rendered verbatim, brackets included, since
// several of projects.json's own values are still literal placeholders
// like "[Solo or team]" (content.md's own rule: "Anything in [brackets]
// is still missing and should stay visible as a placeholder" - plain
// text already does that with no extra styling needed). `thumbnail` is
// still a flat placeholder box, not a visible label - this project's
// own locked convention for missing photography.
//
// Accessibility rebuild: this used to be one giant `<a>` wrapping every
// field as plain `<span>`s with a single `aria-label` summarizing only
// the title+oneLiner - a screen reader read the whole card as one
// run-on ("Plan AAn app that...") with team/role never announced at all
// (they were aria-hidden, redundant with an aria-label that didn't
// mention them). Case-study detail pages don't exist yet (`href` always
// resolved to "#", which goes nowhere), so the card is no longer a link
// at all - a plain `<article>`, title as a real `<h3>`, and every field
// its own element with normal spacing, so it reads the way it looks.
// The hairline structure (class names, data-tag, the overlay markup)
// stays exactly as it was so re-enabling the link later, once detail
// pages exist, is a one-line change: swap the root tag back to `<a
// href={href}>`. The old circular "go" arrow is removed for now too -
// it signaled a click affordance a non-clickable card no longer has;
// SelectedWork.astro's own custom cursor (unaffected by this change)
// already carries that same "go" meaning while the section as a whole
// is being previewed.
import Tag from './Tag.astro';

interface Props {
  order: number;
  title: string;
  tag: string;
  oneLiner: string;
  team: string;
  role: string;
  class?: string;
}

const { order, title, tag, oneLiner, team, role, class: className } = Astro.props;

const number = String(order).padStart(2, '0');
---

<article class:list={['project-card', className]} data-tag={tag}>
  <span class="project-card__image">
    <!-- TODO: replace with a real project image once photography exists (thumbnail: "[image]" in projects.json). -->
  </span>

  <span class="project-card__number" aria-hidden="true">
    {number}
  </span>

  <div class="project-card__overlay">
    <div class="project-card__meta">
      <Tag>{tag}</Tag>
      <span class="project-card__team">{team}</span>
    </div>
    <h3 class="project-card__title">{title}</h3>
    <p class="project-card__line">{oneLiner}</p>
    <p class="project-card__role">{role}</p>
  </div>
</article>

<script>
  // Mouse-magnetic image shift, per direct request ("like a lot of
  // Awwwards type websites, the images kind of shift to the mouse
  // position") - the image drifts a few px toward wherever the cursor
  // sits within the card, on top of the existing scale-up. Gated on
  // `pointer: fine` as well as reduced motion, same as the Gallery
  // cursor/spotlight - this is a continuous mouse-follow effect, not
  // something touch has an equivalent of. The CSS `:hover { transform:
  // scale(1.03) }` rule stays in the stylesheet as the reduced-motion/
  // no-JS fallback: GSAP's inline `transform` always wins over it once
  // this script runs, but when it doesn't (reduced motion, or JS never
  // loads), the plain CSS hover state still gives a functional response,
  // just without the mouse-follow.
  import { gsap, prefersReducedMotion } from '../../utils/motion';

  if (!prefersReducedMotion() && window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches) {
    // Total px range the image can travel corner-to-corner within the
    // card - modest on purpose (the card clips via overflow: hidden, and
    // the hover scale-up is what gives the image the few px of buffer to
    // shift within without revealing the card's own background at an
    // edge).
    const SHIFT_RANGE = 20;

    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.project-card').forEach((card) => {
      const image = card.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.project-card__image');
      if (!image) return;

      const moveX = gsap.quickTo(image, 'x', { duration: 0.5, ease: 'power3.out' });
      const moveY = gsap.quickTo(image, 'y', { duration: 0.5, ease: 'power3.out' });

      card.addEventListener('pointerenter', () => {
        gsap.to(image, { scale: 1.06, duration: 0.5, ease: 'power2.out' });
      });

      card.addEventListener('pointermove', (event) => {
        const rect = card.getBoundingClientRect();
        const relX = (event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width - 0.5;
        const relY = (event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height - 0.5;
        moveX(relX * SHIFT_RANGE);
        moveY(relY * SHIFT_RANGE);
      });

      card.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => {
        gsap.to(image, { x: 0, y: 0, scale: 1, duration: 0.6, ease: 'power3.out' });
      });
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .project-card {
    display: block;
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--color-bg-primary);
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-text-secondary);
    border-radius: var(--radius-image);
    /* --motion-easing-soft, not the plain --motion-easing/ease-out this
                                                         used before, per direct feedback that the card's hover movement
                                                         read as "very harsh." */
    transition:
      transform 0.55s var(--motion-easing-soft),
      border-color var(--motion-duration) var(--motion-easing-soft);
  }

  /* The lift reads as the card physically rising off the page - kept as a
     plain CSS transition (not GSAP) since it's a simple two-state toggle
     on the card itself, an element the mouse-shift script above never
     touches. No drop shadow (removed per earlier direct feedback) - the
     lift, the border darkening to ink, and the overlay sliding into view
     together already carry the "this one's active" read. Hover only, not
     focus-visible - the card is a plain <article> now (see the file
     header comment), not a focusable link, so there's no keyboard focus
     state to style. */
  .project-card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-6px);
    border-color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .project-card__image {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: var(--color-bg-secondary);
    transition: transform var(--motion-duration-slow) var(--motion-easing-soft);
  }

  /* Reduced-motion/no-JS fallback only - the script above takes over
     transform entirely (scale + mouse-follow shift) once it runs, and
     GSAP's inline transform always wins over this rule regardless of
     source order, so there's no double-scaling risk between the two. */
  .project-card:hover .project-card__image {
    transform: scale(1.03);
  }

  .project-card__number {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.85rem;
    left: 0.85rem;
    z-index: 2;
    padding: 0.3em 0.55em;
    background: var(--color-bg-primary);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  /* The info panel - hidden entirely below the card's own bottom edge at
     rest (translateY(100%) moves it a full panel-height down, not just
     opacity: 0, so it can never be read as a sliver or catch a stray
     click while "closed"), sliding up to cover the card's own bottom
     portion on hover/focus. Solid porcelain (not a dark scrim over the
     image) so it reads consistently with the rest of the site's palette
     regardless of what the image underneath eventually is. */
  .project-card__overlay {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 2;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: var(--space-16) var(--space-24);
    background: var(--color-bg-primary);
    border-top: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-text-secondary);
    transform: translateY(100%);
    transition: transform 0.5s var(--motion-easing-soft);
  }

  .project-card:hover .project-card__overlay {
    transform: translateY(0);
  }

  .project-card__meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .project-card__team {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .project-card__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* content.md's own "one-liner" - a real sentence, not a label, so it
     gets the body font at a small size rather than the mono/uppercase
     treatment the rest of the overlay uses. */
  .project-card__line {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .project-card__role {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  /* Touch has no hover to reveal the overlay with - drops out of the
     overlay pattern entirely rather than leaving the info permanently
     unreachable. The image gets its own aspect-ratio and returns to
     normal flow above the panel, which becomes a plain, always-visible,
     non-overlapping block below it - the same "always show the facts, no
     gesture required" fallback already used for the arrow icon, just
     covering the whole panel instead of one glyph. */
  @media (hover: none) {
    .project-card {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
    }

    .project-card__image {
      position: relative;
      aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    }

    .project-card__overlay {
      position: static;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
